A square matrix is singular if and only if its determinant is zero. A singular matrix has no multiplicative inverse; a nonsingular square matrix has one.

The matching first two rows do not determine the determinant by themselves. Expand each determinant along the top row:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(P) &= 1\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 3 & 4\end{vmatrix}
-2\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 4\end{vmatrix}
+1\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 3 & 3\end{vmatrix} \\
&= (4-9)-2(8-9)+(6-3) \\
&= -5+2+3 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
$$

The changed entry appears in the first two minors for $Q$, so those minors must be evaluated again:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(Q) &= 1\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 3 & 5\end{vmatrix}
-2\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 5\end{vmatrix}
+1\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\ 3 & 3\end{vmatrix} \\
&= (5-9)-2(10-9)+(6-3) \\
&= -4-2+3 \\
&= -3.
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $P$ is singular and has no multiplicative inverse. Since $\det(Q)=-3\ne0$, $Q$ is not singular and has a multiplicative inverse. A negative determinant is still nonzero, and $\det(Q)$ is $-3$, not $3$.

Select “$P$ is a singular matrix” and “$Q$ has a multiplicative inverse, but $P$ does not.”

A square matrix is singular if and only if its determinant is zero.

Expand along the top row, using the signs $+,-,+$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(A) &= 0\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\ 3 & 4\end{vmatrix}
-1\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\ 2 & 4\end{vmatrix}
+2\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\ 2 & 3\end{vmatrix} \\
&= 0-1(4-6)+2(3-4) \\
&= 2-2 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, $A$ is singular. Select $\det(A)=0$ and “$A$ is a singular matrix.” Do not select “$A$ is not a singular matrix.”
